---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Markdown: The Language of AI - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="markdown">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        If there's one skill that will immediately improve your experience with
        AI tools, it's learning Markdown. Every major AI assistant — Claude,
        ChatGPT, Copilot — reads and writes Markdown natively. It's how they
        format their responses, how they were trained, and it's how they expect structured input. You
        don't need to be an expert, but knowing the basics makes everything
        smoother.
      </p>

      <h3>Why Markdown?</h3>

      <p>
        Markdown is a lightweight way to add structure and formatting to plain
        text. It was designed to be readable even in its raw form — no angle
        brackets, no complex syntax. When you write a prompt with clear
        headings, bullet lists, and code blocks, the AI can parse your intent
        more accurately. And when the AI responds, it uses Markdown to organize
        its output.
      </p>

      <p>
        Beyond AI, Markdown is everywhere in software development: GitHub
        READMEs, Jira tickets, Confluence pages, Slack messages, pull request
        descriptions. Learning it once pays off across every tool you use.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's also the format of the instruction files this guide keeps coming
        back to. The <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files that
        tools read automatically, and the skills you'll write later, are all just
        Markdown. The same basics you use in a prompt are the same basics you use
        to document a project for the AI.
      </p>

      <h3>The Essentials</h3>

      <p>
        Here's the small subset of Markdown that covers 90% of what you'll use
        with AI tools:
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        <div class="bg-stone-50 px-5 py-3 border-b border-stone-200">
          <div
            class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider"
          >
            Markdown basics
          </div>
        </div>
        <div
          class="px-5 py-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre bg-white overflow-x-auto"
        >{`# Heading 1
## Heading 2
### Heading 3

**Bold text** for emphasis
*Italic text* for lighter emphasis

- Bullet list item
- Another item
  - Nested item

1. Numbered list
2. Second item

\`inline code\` for short references

\`\`\`
code block for
multiple lines of code
\`\`\`

> Blockquote for calling out
> important information

[Link text](https://example.com)

| Column   | Another  |
| -------- | -------- |
| Cell one | Cell two |
| Cell A   | Cell B   |`}
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        That's genuinely it for everyday use. Headings to structure your
        prompts, bold for emphasis, lists for multiple items, code blocks when
        you're sharing code, and tables when you need to compare things
        side by side. You'll be surprised how much clearer your AI interactions
        become once you start structuring your prompts this way.
      </p>

      <h3>Structured Prompts Get Better Results</h3>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-2 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            Unstructured prompt
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed font-mono">
            I need a function that validates email addresses. It should check
            the format and also check if the domain has MX records. Use
            TypeScript. It should return an object with isValid and reason
            fields. Also add tests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2">
            Structured with Markdown
          </div>
          <div
            class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed font-mono whitespace-pre"
          >
{`## Task
Write an email validation function in TypeScript.

## Requirements
- Check format against RFC 5322
- Verify domain has MX records
- Return \`{ isValid: boolean, reason: string }\`

## Tests
Include unit tests covering:
- Valid addresses
- Invalid format
- Valid format but no MX record`}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Same information, but the structured version is easier for both you and
        the AI to parse. The AI is less likely to miss a requirement when each
        one is its own bullet point.
      </p>

      <h3>Learn More</h3>

      <p>
        If you're new to Markdown or want a refresher, these resources will get
        you up to speed quickly:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-3 my-8">
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <a
              href="https://www.markdowntutorial.com/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="font-semibold"
              >Markdown Tutorial</a
            > — an interactive, hands-on tutorial you can complete in your browser
            in about 10 minutes. The best place to start if you've never used Markdown.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <a
              href="https://www.markdownguide.org/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="font-semibold"
              >Markdown Guide</a
            > — a comprehensive reference you can bookmark and come back to. Covers
            basic syntax, extended syntax, and tool-specific variations.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <a
              href="https://commonmark.org/help/tutorial/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="font-semibold"
              >CommonMark Tutorial</a
            > — another interactive tutorial with practice exercises after each lesson.
            Good if you want to make sure you've actually retained it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="You already know some of this">
        <p>
          If you've ever written a GitHub README, formatted a Slack message with
          asterisks, or edited a Jira ticket description — you've used Markdown.
          It's the same syntax everywhere, with minor variations. Ten minutes
          with the tutorial above and you'll have it locked in.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
